# Design Thinking Tools

This rule preserves the old non-coding design and exploration skills. It must not
override `matt-skills-core.mdc` for programming, debugging, tests, commits, PRs,
or implementation work.

## Scope

Use these skills for idea shaping, design discussion, strategic exploration,
problem framing, and skill/process documentation.

If the task asks to modify code, debug code, write tests, create PRDs/issues, or
commit work, route through `matt-skills-core.mdc` instead.

Negative implementation intent wins here: `不要编码`, `暂不编码`, `先不实现`,
`只讨论方案`, and similar phrases mean the user wants exploration only.

## Restored Non-Coding Routes

- Rough idea, product concept, design discussion, requirements exploration -> `superpowers/collaboration/brainstorming`
- Two valid approaches are in tension and should not be collapsed too early -> `superpowers/architecture/preserving-productive-tensions`
- Stuck because assumptions feel fixed -> `superpowers/problem-solving/inversion-exercise`
- Need to force unrelated concepts together for new insight -> `superpowers/problem-solving/collision-zone-thinking`
- Pattern appears across multiple domains -> `superpowers/problem-solving/meta-pattern-recognition`
- Need to test an idea at extreme scale -> `superpowers/problem-solving/scale-game`
- Complexity is spiraling and one simplifying insight may delete many pieces -> `superpowers/problem-solving/simplification-cascades`
- Stuck and unsure which thinking technique fits -> `superpowers/problem-solving/when-stuck`
- Need to understand why an idea or approach evolved over time -> `superpowers/research/tracing-knowledge-lineages`
- Need to remember relevant past conversations during non-coding exploration -> `superpowers/collaboration/remembering-conversations`
- Creating, testing, maintaining, or sharing skills themselves -> `superpowers/meta/*`

## Separation Contract

- Do not restore old Superpowers coding workflow skills as defaults.
- Do not call old `writing-plans`, `executing-plans`, `systematic-debugging`,
  `test-driven-development`, `requesting-code-review`, or `finishing-a-development-branch`
  for normal programming work.
- Matt coding skills remain the core programming system.
